.TH WSYS 4
.SH NAME
wsys \- window system, first prototype (processor backend)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wsys
[
.B -s
.I srvname
]
[
.B -i
.I cmd
]
[
.B -x
]
.PP
.B mount /srv/wsys.$user /mnt/wsys
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wsys
is the window system of the
.B ui
branch, a successor of
.IR rio (1)
in the making.
It draws on the screen
.IR draw (3)
gives it (or in the window of
.BR /dev/winname ),
posts a 9P connection as
.BI /srv/ srvname
(default
.BR wsys.$user )
and serves a tree of windows.
With
.BR -i ,
it runs
.I cmd
with
.IR rc (1)
in a name space of its own with the tree on
.B /mnt/wsys
and
.B $wsys
naming the service;
.B -x
makes it exit when
.I cmd
does.
.PP
A window's pixels are not sent through the files: they are in a global
segment of
.IR segment (3)
that
.I wsys
makes at an address it picks, one per window size; the client attaches it by
name with
.IR segattach (2)
and gets the same address.
The layout is
.B x8r8g8b8
(32 bits a pixel, little-endian: blue, green, red, unused),
a line every
.I stride
bytes.
.TP
.B clone
Opening it makes a window; the open file is then the window's
.BR ctl ,
and the window lives until it is closed.
.TP
.IB n /ctl
Reading gives one line of fields:
.I n
.I x0 y0 x1 y1
(the window's content on the screen)
.I w h stride
.I segment
.I address
.I generation
.I focus
(1 if it has the focus).
Messages written:
.RS
.TP
.BI rect " x0 y0 x1 y1
moves the window; a new size makes a new segment
.RI ( generation
counts them); the client detaches the old one at the
.B r
event and attaches the one
.B ctl
names.
.TP
.BR present " [\fIx0 y0 x1 y1\fP]"
the pixels of the rectangle (in the window's coordinates; all of them without one) go to the screen.
.TP
.B top
raises the window and gives it the focus.
.TP
.BI label " text
.TP
.B delete
.RE
.TP
.IB n /event
Reading blocks until there are events; each is a line:
.BI m " x y buttons msec
(the mouse, in the window's coordinates, while it is over the window or a button is held),
.BI k " rune
(a key, the rune in hexadecimal),
.BI r " w h generation
(a new size),
.BI f " focus
(1: the window got the focus, 0: lost it).
.PP
.I Wsys
composes on the processor through
.IR draw (3):
a background, the windows bottom to top, each with a border (blue with the focus).
Mouse events come from
.BR /dev/mouse ;
a button pressed over a window gives it the focus and raises it.
Keys come from
.B /dev/cons
in raw mode and go to the focused window.
.SH EXAMPLES
A client in C: see
.BR /tests/ui/wsystest.c .
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/wsys
(Rust;
.BR scripts/build/cargo.sh )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR rio (1),
.IR draw (3),
.IR segment (3)
.SH BUGS
A prototype: no window management by mouse (moving, sweeping), no labels
shown, no Vulkan backend yet, no
.IR draw (3)
for old programs in its windows.
The segments belong to the user
.I wsys
runs as; clients must be that user (or the host owner).
Keys are read from
.BR /dev/cons :
another reader of it (an idle shell on the same console) takes some.
